  author        = {Abbas Ashour Alaraza, Hayder and Kharun, Makhmud and Chiadighikaobi, Paschal Chimeremeze},
  year          = 2022,
  journal       = {Cogent Engineering},
  volume        = 9,
  number        = 1,
  doi           = {10.1080/23311916.2022.2136603},
  url           = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85140986693&doi=10.1080%2F23311916.2022.2136603&partnerID=40&md5=c615ee708a5672e844278d79c7a6e902},
  note          = {Cited by: 12; All Open Access; Gold Open Access; Green Open Access},
  abstract      = {Basalt fiber (BF) is one of the most important sustainable materials used to enhance high-performance concrete (HPC) in recent years. There are two types of BF, namely micro-BF and macro-BF. Despite that, macro-BF is a new material and there are few studies about it. A variety of standard material tests were carried out to examine the impact of MiniBars BF as well as its fractions (0.6–1.8\%) on the physical and mechanical properties of HPC, including compressive strength (Formula presented.), flexural strength ((Formula presented.)), workability, and modulus of elasticity ((Formula presented.)). The study applied the one variable at a time method by using an identical mix with different fractions of MiniBars BF to minimize the influence of other factors on the result of the test. Six different mixes were investigated. The experiment showed that the MiniBars BF fraction slightly affected the workability of the HPC mix. The results indicated that the small amount of MiniBars BF did not affect the (Formula presented.) and the (Formula presented.) of HPC. But it has a negative effect after the dosage of MiniBars BF increased by more than 1.2\%. The MiniBars BF enhanced the (Formula presented.) significantly even with a small percentage until it reached the optimum effect at 0.9\% then drop slightly. \textcopyright{} 2022 The Author(s). This open access article is distributed under a Creative Commons Attribution (CC-BY) 4.0 license.},
  author_keywords = {Basalt fiber reinforced concrete; compressive strength; flexure strength; high-performance concrete; minibars; modulus of elasticity},
