  note          = {Cited by: 0},
  abstract      = {This chapter examines how computational infrastructure and software tools condition the feasibility of robust anomaly detection in modern econometric analysis. As economic data environments expand in scale, dimensionality, and complexity, robustness can no longer be treated solely as a statistical property independent of computational constraints. The chapter analyzes how algorithmic frameworks, approximation techniques, simulation- based validation, benchmarking practices, and reproducible workflows operationalize robust methods in large- scale empirical settings, with particular attention to parallel, distributed, and high- performance computing environments. By linking statistical robustness with scalable and transparent computational design, the chapter highlights the inferential, organizational, and governance implications of computationally intensive anomaly detection, positioning computation as a core methodological component of sustainable and responsible econometric practice. \textcopyright{} 2026 by IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved.},
