  note          = {Cited by: 0; All Open Access; Gold Open Access},
  abstract      = {The latest Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) report describes the unprecedented rate at which the global climate has warmed in the last 200 years (U.N. News,), resulting in ocean temperature increases, rising sea levels, intense rains and floods, new records for heat waves and droughts, and ever-growing stress on freshwater availability. This is a critical, yet exciting time for climate science with access to a wide variety of global, regional, and local observations and advanced scenario-based forecast and analysis tools to improve our understanding on what climate change phenomena can be attributed to the current crisis and render mitigation opportunities to improve future outcomes. To address our big data analytic challenges, the solution cannot rely simply on advanced computing infrastructure or a few promising technology components. It requires carefully architected end-to-end solutions that are sustainable over time and satisfy operational requirements, often constrained by the available budget. The chapters in this section present some architectural solutions for these big data challenges, addressing key elements of big data analysis architecture. \textcopyright{} 2023 American Geophysical Union.},
