  title         = {INTEGRATING SOCIO-EMOTIONAL LEARNING AND CULTURE OF PEACE THROUGH HOLISTIC ASSESSMENT: Insight from NEP, 2020},
  author        = {Kamal, Peeyush},
  year          = 2026,
  journal       = {Peace Education: A Framework for Implementation},
  pages         = {184--195},
  doi           = {10.4324/9781003734178-16},
  url           = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105032588929&doi=10.4324%2F9781003734178-16&partnerID=40&md5=2b95109aab5bb444565db91657732790},
  note          = {Cited by: 0},
  abstract      = {This chapter examines how the Holistic Progress Card (HPC), which is part of NCERT's Performance Assessment, Review, and Analysis of Knowledge for Holistic Development (PARAKH) initiative, integrates soft skills and peace education is one of them. In keeping with the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) of the UN, the chapter highlights the importance of peace education as a decisive element in creating a society that is equitable, inclusive, and peaceful. It emphasizes the goal of the National Education Policy (NEP) 2020, which advocates for a well-rounded educational approach that emphasizes developing students' emotional, social, and ethical faculties in addition to their academic knowledge. The HPC incorporates socio-emotional skills like empathy, conflict resolution, and teamwork, as well as emotional intelligence and ethical values, to evaluate students' overall development beyond academic performance. The HPC's competency-based, activity-oriented approach to assessment is one of the features examined in the chapter. This approach creates a learning environment that supports the development of 21st-century skills that are essential for peacebuilding. It also talks about HPC's role in advancing peace education, stressing the value of cultivating principles like social responsibility, equity, and nonviolence. The chapter also discusses the difficulties in putting this new assessment framework into practice and offers solutions for its successful incorporation into the Indian educational system. \textcopyright{} 2026 selection and editorial matter Lokanath Mishra individual chapters the contributors.},
