  note          = {Cited by: 0},
  abstract      = {Digital manufacturing science involves applying advanced computer-oriented technologies into manufacturing to improve creativity, flexibility, operation, automation, and efficiency in manufacturing systems. This emerging area of study draws from information and operations management, industrial engineering, computer science, and computational mathematics to predict, redesign, and control manufacturing systems and processes. Using digital twins, engineers can track and assess the processes of manufacturing in real time, thus enabling scheduling of maintenance the moment issues start emerging and instant decision-making. Digital manufacturing makes it easier to adopt Internet of Things to improve flow of communication between machines and devices. Real-time data are collected during the production process, and machine learning helps to process the collected data, find patterns, and make improvements to the production. However, the transformation to additive manufacturing and advance robotics is viewed as a testament to the changes brought by digital manufacturing science where customization and creation of geometry that may be impossible by conventional methodologies are enabled. That is, this merger of computations and manufacturing enables sustainable accomplishment of key organizational targets complemented by efficiency in a globally competitive environment. Computing manufacturing in digital manufacturing science translates to a revolution in manufacturing improvement strategies and solutions through the provision of industrial ``road maps'' to effectively respond to such challenges, as well as make the most of potential benefits in the rapidly technologizing world. With future advancements in technology being developed, the way it will affect manufacturing processes will redesign various manufacturing norms and work toward improvements. \textcopyright{} 2026 Scrivener Publishing LLC.},
