  year          = 2011,
  journal       = {Plant Archives},
  volume        = 11,
  number        = 2,
  pages         = {887--890},
  url           = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84861076524&partnerID=40&md5=6e89ce7eaa5ebce02bfb900cd3368e8b},
  note          = {Cited by: 2},
  abstract      = {Concrete is one of the most widely used construction materials in the world. First, it consumes huge quantities of virgin materials. Second, the principal binder in concrete is portland cement, the production of which is a major contributor to greenhouse gas emissions that are implicated in global warming and climate change. Therefore the concrete construction industry is not sustainable. On the other hand most of the industrial wastes disposed off to an open area of dumping yards or to the river nearby. These industrial wastes lead to the pollution of water bodies and loss of productive land. Environmental issues are playing an important role in the sustainable development of industries and agricultural fields. To safeguard the environment, efforts are being made for recycling different wastes and utilize them in value added applications. A sustainable concrete can be made with industrial wastes such as silica fume and bottom ash as the replacement material for cement and fine aggregate respectively. Use of such industrial wastes can reduce the consumption Portland cement clinker and natural resource sand. Moreover, this eco-friendly concrete make the concrete industry as sustainable, improves its strength and durability and helps to agriculture by minimizing the pollution from industrial wastes. In this paper, an optimum level of bottom ash and silica fume for making a High Performance Concrete (HPC) mix was determined and its compressive strength was studied.},
  author_keywords = {Agriculture; Compressive strength; Concrete; Industrial wastes},
  type          = {Article},
  publication_stage = {Final},
  source        = {Scopus}
}
