  note          = {Cited by: 1},
  abstract      = {Heterotrophic plate count (HPC) is a nonspecific method for measuring the number of viable, indigenous bacteria in water. HPC is a useful indicator of water quality and can be used to establish the presence of residual disinfectant and microbial regrowth. A maximum contaminant level has not been established under the Safe Drinking Water Act. Control of elevated HPC levels associated with point-of-use drinking-water treatment units was investigated. Heterotrophic plate counts on product water from reverse-osmosis drinking- water treatment units with and without carbon filters were collected. Regular water usage, to prevent stagnation conditions, was found to be a significant factor in preventing elevated HPC levels.},
