  note          = {Cited by: 0},
  abstract      = {Ultra-high performance concrete (UHPC) is typically based on ordinary Portland cement (OPC), yet it can also be developed using alkali-activated materials (AAM). As AAMs yield reaction products differing from OPC, various durability-related properties in the solid state can be attained. AAM-UHPC, owing to its substitution with granulated blast furnace slag, exhibits a diminished climate footprint compared to OPC-UHPC. Nonetheless, AAM-UHPC necessitates the utilization of alkali silicate solution in high concentrations, thereby negatively impacting its climate footprint. Nevertheless, the overall footprint is mitigated relative to OPC-UHPC, particularly concerning the CO<sub>2</sub>- footprint. In pursuit of further reduction, the AAM-UHPC formulation underwent adjustments involving the reduction of binder proportion, activator concentration and silica fume content. Although these alterations led to a decrease in compressive strength performance, a material possessing adequate properties for construction applications was achieved, accompanied by a significant reduction in its climate footprint. \textcopyright{} f\'{e}d\'{e}ration internationale du b\'{e}ton (fib).},
